WEBVTT

00:00:00.430 --> 00:00:01.969
From far away, Earth looks calm:

00:00:02.019 --> 00:00:04.848
a blue and white marble turning

00:00:04.898 --> 00:00:06.266
quietly in the dark.

00:00:06.316 --> 00:00:08.677
But up close, it never sits still.

00:00:08.727 --> 00:00:11.727
For 4.6 billion years, it has been boiling,

00:00:11.777 --> 00:00:14.250
freezing and reshaping itself.

00:00:14.300 --> 00:00:16.620
Welcome to the wonders of planet Earth.

00:00:17.370 --> 00:00:20.240
Our journey has seventeen stops:

00:00:20.290 --> 00:00:22.440
from rocks falling out of space

00:00:22.490 --> 00:00:24.070
to the deepest ocean,

00:00:24.120 --> 00:00:27.331
the wildest winds and the driest deserts.

00:00:30.781 --> 00:00:33.901
Every day, lumps of rock and metal

00:00:33.951 --> 00:00:36.688
from space slam into our atmosphere.

00:00:36.738 --> 00:00:39.451
Friction makes them glow,

00:00:39.501 --> 00:00:41.421
and we see them as meteors,

00:00:41.471 --> 00:00:44.262
shooting stars, or blazing fireballs.

00:00:45.012 --> 00:00:47.482
In November 1833, the most brilliant

00:00:47.532 --> 00:00:50.178
meteor shower ever recorded lit up

00:00:50.228 --> 00:00:52.932
the skies over the United States.

00:00:52.982 --> 00:00:54.797
Many people woke up convinced

00:00:54.847 --> 00:00:56.362
the world was ending.

00:00:57.112 --> 00:01:00.222
About 66 million years ago, a giant space

00:01:00.272 --> 00:01:03.592
rock struck near Chicxulub, in Mexico.

00:01:03.642 --> 00:01:05.463
Its dust blocked the sunlight,

00:01:05.513 --> 00:01:07.512
and most animal species,

00:01:07.562 --> 00:01:09.933
including the dinosaurs, died out.

00:01:10.683 --> 00:01:12.580
Such an impact heats the air

00:01:12.630 --> 00:01:15.173
to around 60,000 degrees Celsius, ten

00:01:15.223 --> 00:01:17.753
times hotter than the surface of the Sun.

00:01:17.803 --> 00:01:19.743
The largest known scar,

00:01:19.793 --> 00:01:21.953
the Vredefort crater in South Africa,

00:01:22.003 --> 00:01:24.333
is about 300 kilometres across.

00:01:25.083 --> 00:01:26.903
Some visitors are beautiful.

00:01:26.953 --> 00:01:28.553
Particles blown from the Sun

00:01:28.603 --> 00:01:31.463
on the solar wind hit Earth's magnetic field

00:01:31.513 --> 00:01:33.770
and light up the polar skies with green

00:01:33.820 --> 00:01:34.964
and violet aurorae.

00:01:35.714 --> 00:01:38.426
Comets are chunks of ice and rock

00:01:38.476 --> 00:01:40.824
that blaze as they near the Sun.

00:01:40.874 --> 00:01:44.201
In 1996, Comet Hyakutake passed close

00:01:44.251 --> 00:01:46.880
to Earth, and it will not return

00:01:46.930 --> 00:01:49.475
for tens of thousands of years.

00:01:51.325 --> 00:01:53.441
To see why our planet shakes,

00:01:53.491 --> 00:01:55.115
travel to its heart.

00:01:55.165 --> 00:01:57.623
The core reaches about 5,400 degrees

00:01:57.673 --> 00:02:00.915
Celsius, as hot as the surface of the Sun,

00:02:00.965 --> 00:02:02.178
and that heat keeps

00:02:02.228 --> 00:02:04.195
the rock above on the move.

00:02:04.945 --> 00:02:08.225
Earth is built in layers: a solid iron core,

00:02:08.275 --> 00:02:11.605
a liquid outer core, a slowly flowing mantle,

00:02:11.655 --> 00:02:13.745
and the thin crust we live on.

00:02:13.795 --> 00:02:16.866
As the mantle churns, the crust cracks.

00:02:17.616 --> 00:02:19.385
Those cracks split the surface

00:02:19.435 --> 00:02:21.246
into giant tectonic plates.

00:02:21.296 --> 00:02:23.546
They drift a few centimetres every year,

00:02:23.596 --> 00:02:25.346
and where they meet or pull apart,

00:02:25.396 --> 00:02:26.966
the Earth gets dramatic.

00:02:27.716 --> 00:02:30.106
Around the Pacific lies the Ring of Fire,

00:02:30.156 --> 00:02:32.369
a horseshoe of plate boundaries holding

00:02:32.419 --> 00:02:33.545
more than 75 percent

00:02:33.595 --> 00:02:34.766
of the world's volcanoes

00:02:34.816 --> 00:02:36.897
and its most violent earthquakes.

00:02:37.647 --> 00:02:39.377
Beneath the Atlantic runs

00:02:39.427 --> 00:02:40.897
the Mid-Atlantic Ridge,

00:02:40.947 --> 00:02:43.407
about 16,000 kilometres long.

00:02:43.457 --> 00:02:45.847
Here, lava rises through the gap,

00:02:45.897 --> 00:02:47.790
new seafloor forms, and the ocean grows

00:02:47.840 --> 00:02:50.057
a little wider every year.

00:02:50.807 --> 00:02:53.527
In Iceland, the ridge rises above the waves.

00:02:53.577 --> 00:02:54.873
This island of fire

00:02:54.923 --> 00:02:57.517
and ice is slowly being pulled apart,

00:02:57.567 --> 00:02:59.448
between North America and Europe.

00:03:00.198 --> 00:03:03.698
In 2010, Iceland's Eyjafjallajökull blasted

00:03:03.748 --> 00:03:06.129
ash 11 kilometres into the sky.

00:03:06.179 --> 00:03:08.554
The cloud drifted over Europe,

00:03:08.604 --> 00:03:10.848
and for about a week,

00:03:10.898 --> 00:03:12.839
air travel came to a standstill.

00:03:14.689 --> 00:03:17.886
When India crashed into Asia, it pushed up

00:03:17.936 --> 00:03:20.679
the Himalayas, which are still rising today.

00:03:20.729 --> 00:03:24.279
At their crown stands the highest mountain

00:03:24.329 --> 00:03:26.589
on Earth: Chomolungma,

00:03:26.639 --> 00:03:28.479
Sagarmatha, or Mount Everest.

00:03:29.229 --> 00:03:32.103
In the 1800s, British surveyors in India

00:03:32.153 --> 00:03:34.808
found that a remote summit was taller

00:03:34.858 --> 00:03:36.445
than any mountain ever measured.

00:03:36.495 --> 00:03:38.682
In 1865, it was named

00:03:38.732 --> 00:03:40.510
after Sir George Everest.

00:03:41.260 --> 00:03:44.730
Its exact height is still debated,

00:03:44.780 --> 00:03:46.137
because snow, ice

00:03:46.187 --> 00:03:49.420
and survey methods all change the answer.

00:03:49.470 --> 00:03:53.094
In 2020, China and Nepal agreed

00:03:53.144 --> 00:03:54.930
on 8,848.86 metres.

00:03:55.680 --> 00:03:58.110
On 29 May 1953, Edmund Hillary

00:03:58.160 --> 00:04:00.400
and Tenzing Norgay became the first

00:04:00.450 --> 00:04:03.670
people to stand on top of the world.

00:04:03.720 --> 00:04:05.660
Thousands have followed since.

00:04:06.410 --> 00:04:08.930
Climbers spend weeks at Base Camp,

00:04:08.980 --> 00:04:12.330
5,400 metres up, adjusting to the thin air.

00:04:12.380 --> 00:04:15.570
Above lies the deadly Khumbu Icefall,

00:04:15.620 --> 00:04:18.240
crossed with ropes and aluminium ladders.

00:04:18.990 --> 00:04:21.310
Then they climb through a chain of camps,

00:04:21.360 --> 00:04:23.880
from 6,000 to 8,000 metres,

00:04:23.930 --> 00:04:26.439
past the Geneva Spur and the giant

00:04:26.489 --> 00:04:28.571
neighbours Lhotse and Nuptse.

00:04:29.321 --> 00:04:32.721
Above 8,000 metres is the Death Zone.

00:04:32.771 --> 00:04:33.849
Each breath holds only

00:04:33.899 --> 00:04:36.331
a third of the oxygen at sea level,

00:04:36.381 --> 00:04:38.278
and climbers cling to the ice

00:04:38.328 --> 00:04:39.962
with axes and crampons.

00:04:40.712 --> 00:04:42.498
Sadly, Everest is also

00:04:42.548 --> 00:04:45.062
the world's highest garbage dump.

00:04:45.112 --> 00:04:48.962
And it has neighbours: K2, Kangchenjunga,

00:04:49.012 --> 00:04:50.843
Lhotse and Makalu complete

00:04:50.893 --> 00:04:53.513
the five tallest peaks on Earth.

00:04:55.363 --> 00:04:58.553
When the ground splits open, molten rock,

00:04:58.603 --> 00:05:01.043
ash and gas burst out.

00:05:01.093 --> 00:05:03.338
Scientists rate eruptions on a scale

00:05:03.388 --> 00:05:06.255
that runs from gentle all the way up

00:05:06.305 --> 00:05:07.633
to mega-colossal.

00:05:08.383 --> 00:05:09.599
Inside a volcano,

00:05:09.649 --> 00:05:12.623
magma gathers in deep chambers.

00:05:12.673 --> 00:05:14.135
Heat and pressure force it up

00:05:14.185 --> 00:05:16.393
a central pipe to the crater,

00:05:16.443 --> 00:05:19.204
or sideways through vents in its flanks.

00:05:19.954 --> 00:05:22.243
In 1980, Mount St. Helens exploded

00:05:22.293 --> 00:05:25.784
so violently that its north side collapsed,

00:05:25.834 --> 00:05:26.796
and the mountain lost

00:05:26.846 --> 00:05:29.604
about 400 metres of height in a single day.

00:05:30.354 --> 00:05:31.884
Lava can move fast.

00:05:31.934 --> 00:05:33.874
At Nyiragongo, in Congo, it raced

00:05:33.924 --> 00:05:36.634
at more than 60 kilometres per hour.

00:05:36.684 --> 00:05:38.304
And when lava meets the ocean,

00:05:38.354 --> 00:05:40.475
the seawater boils into steam.

00:05:41.225 --> 00:05:43.505
Nearly two thousand years ago,

00:05:43.555 --> 00:05:46.329
a volcano in New Zealand blasted out

00:05:46.379 --> 00:05:48.905
about 100 cubic kilometres of rock.

00:05:48.955 --> 00:05:51.795
Its giant crater is now Lake Taupo.

00:05:52.545 --> 00:05:54.155
In AD 79, Mount Vesuvius shot

00:05:54.205 --> 00:05:57.399
ash 33 kilometres into the sky.

00:05:57.449 --> 00:05:59.915
When the column collapsed,

00:05:59.965 --> 00:06:02.466
it buried the Roman city of Pompeii.

00:06:03.216 --> 00:06:05.125
Layer after layer of lava

00:06:05.175 --> 00:06:07.796
and ash builds a volcano's cone.

00:06:07.846 --> 00:06:10.688
In Hawaii, active craters breathe steam

00:06:10.738 --> 00:06:14.057
almost every day, slowly building new land.

00:06:15.907 --> 00:06:16.852
In some places,

00:06:16.902 --> 00:06:19.227
Earth's heat reaches the surface.

00:06:19.277 --> 00:06:21.693
Underground water is heated past boiling

00:06:21.743 --> 00:06:24.279
point and bursts out as scalding steam,

00:06:24.329 --> 00:06:25.937
and even the mud bubbles.

00:06:26.687 --> 00:06:28.448
Old Faithful, in Yellowstone,

00:06:28.498 --> 00:06:30.508
shoots water up to 55 metres high

00:06:30.558 --> 00:06:32.256
about every hour and a half:

00:06:32.306 --> 00:06:34.504
the time its chamber needs to refill

00:06:34.554 --> 00:06:35.628
and heat up again.

00:06:36.378 --> 00:06:39.658
Yellowstone holds about half the world's

00:06:39.708 --> 00:06:41.578
geysers, plus thousands of hot springs.

00:06:41.628 --> 00:06:43.329
And all of it sits inside

00:06:43.379 --> 00:06:45.348
the caldera of a supervolcano.

00:06:46.098 --> 00:06:48.298
Ordinary volcanoes build mountains.

00:06:48.348 --> 00:06:50.788
Supervolcanoes blow them apart.

00:06:50.838 --> 00:06:52.487
Yellowstone's last giant eruption

00:06:52.537 --> 00:06:55.088
was about a thousand times more powerful

00:06:55.138 --> 00:06:56.519
than Mount St. Helens.

00:06:57.269 --> 00:06:58.325
In New Zealand,

00:06:58.375 --> 00:07:00.314
the Waimangu Geyser erupted

00:07:00.364 --> 00:07:03.179
for just four years, starting in 1900.

00:07:03.229 --> 00:07:05.959
Its jets reached 460 metres,

00:07:06.009 --> 00:07:07.979
taller than the Empire State Building.

00:07:08.729 --> 00:07:10.359
In Nagano, Japan, snow monkeys spend

00:07:10.409 --> 00:07:12.501
the freezing winter soaking

00:07:12.551 --> 00:07:14.249
in natural hot springs.

00:07:14.299 --> 00:07:16.141
Some get so comfortable they doze off

00:07:16.191 --> 00:07:16.930
in the water.

00:07:17.680 --> 00:07:20.783
At Pamukkale in Turkey, hot mineral water

00:07:20.833 --> 00:07:23.670
has built gleaming white terraces of stone.

00:07:23.720 --> 00:07:26.321
Its name means Cotton Castle.

00:07:28.171 --> 00:07:30.511
Rock is tough, but brittle.

00:07:30.561 --> 00:07:32.157
Pushed past its breaking point,

00:07:32.207 --> 00:07:34.491
the crust buckles and slips,

00:07:34.541 --> 00:07:36.541
and the ground shakes and shatters.

00:07:37.291 --> 00:07:38.951
This happens along faults,

00:07:39.001 --> 00:07:41.631
where blocks of rock grind past each other.

00:07:41.681 --> 00:07:43.843
They get stuck, pressure builds,

00:07:43.893 --> 00:07:46.091
and suddenly the rock jolts forward:

00:07:46.141 --> 00:07:47.112
an earthquake.

00:07:47.862 --> 00:07:49.204
Where the rock breaks

00:07:49.254 --> 00:07:50.842
underground is the focus.

00:07:50.892 --> 00:07:52.942
The point above it is the epicentre.

00:07:52.992 --> 00:07:55.834
From there, seismic waves race outward,

00:07:55.884 --> 00:07:57.492
shaking towns and cities.

00:07:58.242 --> 00:08:00.805
In 1906, the San Andreas Fault tore

00:08:00.855 --> 00:08:02.612
through San Francisco.

00:08:02.662 --> 00:08:04.252
And in 2010, an earthquake

00:08:04.302 --> 00:08:07.151
in Chile moved the city of Concepción

00:08:07.201 --> 00:08:08.943
about three metres west.

00:08:09.693 --> 00:08:12.300
In 2008, a massive earthquake struck

00:08:12.350 --> 00:08:14.053
Sichuan, in China.

00:08:14.103 --> 00:08:16.693
Roads split, hillsides collapsed,

00:08:16.743 --> 00:08:18.943
and whole towns were reduced to rubble.

00:08:19.693 --> 00:08:21.943
Earthquakes still cannot be predicted,

00:08:21.993 --> 00:08:23.753
but we can build smarter.

00:08:23.803 --> 00:08:25.623
In Japan and California,

00:08:25.673 --> 00:08:27.715
flexible buildings on shock-absorbing

00:08:27.765 --> 00:08:30.254
foundations sway instead of collapsing.

00:08:31.004 --> 00:08:33.274
When a fault slips beneath the sea,

00:08:33.324 --> 00:08:34.984
it can launch a tsunami.

00:08:35.034 --> 00:08:37.686
In 2004, giant waves swept

00:08:37.736 --> 00:08:39.574
across the Indian Ocean,

00:08:39.624 --> 00:08:41.725
from Indonesia to Thailand.

00:08:43.575 --> 00:08:45.782
Rivers can grind rock into dust

00:08:45.832 --> 00:08:48.265
and power entire cities.

00:08:48.315 --> 00:08:50.415
And by almost any measure, one river

00:08:50.465 --> 00:08:52.625
stands above them all: the Amazon.

00:08:53.375 --> 00:08:55.085
It begins high in the Andes,

00:08:55.135 --> 00:08:57.999
where glacier water trickles down

00:08:58.049 --> 00:09:01.064
and streams like the Urubamba River join

00:09:01.114 --> 00:09:03.516
together to form its headwaters.

00:09:04.266 --> 00:09:05.783
Across South America,

00:09:05.833 --> 00:09:08.170
more than 1,100 tributaries feed

00:09:08.220 --> 00:09:11.396
the Amazon, more than any other river,

00:09:11.446 --> 00:09:13.916
some arriving as thundering waterfalls.

00:09:14.666 --> 00:09:16.686
The Nile may be slightly longer,

00:09:16.736 --> 00:09:19.275
but every second, the Amazon pours

00:09:19.325 --> 00:09:21.986
about 43 times as much water into the sea.

00:09:22.036 --> 00:09:24.147
No other river comes close.

00:09:24.897 --> 00:09:26.029
In the wet season,

00:09:26.079 --> 00:09:27.999
its floods spread over an area

00:09:28.049 --> 00:09:30.625
the size of Germany, and its waters feed

00:09:30.675 --> 00:09:32.727
the largest rainforest on Earth.

00:09:33.477 --> 00:09:35.467
Flooded forests line the river,

00:09:35.517 --> 00:09:37.120
and in their muddy channels

00:09:37.170 --> 00:09:39.437
lives one of its most mysterious animals:

00:09:39.487 --> 00:09:40.878
the river dolphin.

00:09:41.628 --> 00:09:43.963
Where the river meets the sea,

00:09:44.013 --> 00:09:45.598
the tide pushes back.

00:09:45.648 --> 00:09:48.576
A long wave called the pororoca rolls

00:09:48.626 --> 00:09:51.757
upstream, and surfers can ride it for up

00:09:51.807 --> 00:09:53.189
to ten kilometres.

00:09:55.039 --> 00:09:56.759
On our restless planet, landscapes

00:09:56.809 --> 00:10:00.069
are always being carved and reshaped,

00:10:00.119 --> 00:10:02.689
too slowly for a human life to notice.

00:10:02.739 --> 00:10:05.189
But the results are all around us.

00:10:05.939 --> 00:10:08.939
In dry places, the wind becomes a sculptor,

00:10:08.989 --> 00:10:10.949
blasting rock with sand.

00:10:10.999 --> 00:10:13.519
In Utah's Arches National Park,

00:10:13.569 --> 00:10:15.095
it has carved stone arches

00:10:15.145 --> 00:10:16.579
and balancing rocks.

00:10:17.329 --> 00:10:20.559
It leaves strange shapes behind: hoodoos,

00:10:20.609 --> 00:10:23.149
tall spires capped with harder stone,

00:10:23.199 --> 00:10:25.999
and in Australia, the Devils Marbles,

00:10:26.049 --> 00:10:28.319
giant boulders balanced on one another.

00:10:29.069 --> 00:10:31.798
Rushing water can cut a deep, narrow crack

00:10:31.848 --> 00:10:34.109
through soft rock: a slot canyon,

00:10:34.159 --> 00:10:36.230
glowing with light from far above.

00:10:36.980 --> 00:10:39.073
The greatest carver of all

00:10:39.123 --> 00:10:40.500
is the Colorado River.

00:10:40.550 --> 00:10:44.010
Over millions of years, it cut

00:10:44.060 --> 00:10:46.550
the Grand Canyon, 446 kilometres long

00:10:46.600 --> 00:10:49.210
and more than 1.6 kilometres deep.

00:10:49.960 --> 00:10:52.628
On the coast, waves armed with sand grind

00:10:52.678 --> 00:10:55.279
the cliffs away, until the undercut rock

00:10:55.329 --> 00:10:57.201
above collapses into the sea.

00:10:59.051 --> 00:11:00.797
Where snow rarely melts,

00:11:00.847 --> 00:11:04.091
it piles up and is squeezed into solid ice.

00:11:04.141 --> 00:11:05.451
Pulled by gravity,

00:11:05.501 --> 00:11:08.201
this ice creeps downhill as a glacier.

00:11:08.951 --> 00:11:10.221
At a glacier's front,

00:11:10.271 --> 00:11:13.001
ice breaks away with a thunderous crack.

00:11:13.051 --> 00:11:15.593
In Argentina, towers of ice crash

00:11:15.643 --> 00:11:17.729
from the Perito Moreno Glacier

00:11:17.779 --> 00:11:19.152
into the lake below.

00:11:19.902 --> 00:11:22.672
When more ice forms than melts,

00:11:22.722 --> 00:11:24.362
a glacier advances.

00:11:24.412 --> 00:11:26.572
When more melts, it retreats.

00:11:26.622 --> 00:11:28.200
Alaska's Hubbard Glacier has

00:11:28.250 --> 00:11:30.852
been advancing for over a century.

00:11:31.602 --> 00:11:32.916
Most glaciers move

00:11:32.966 --> 00:11:35.092
a few metres a day at most.

00:11:35.142 --> 00:11:37.990
But Greenland's Jakobshavn Glacier flows

00:11:38.040 --> 00:11:39.883
at up to 20 metres a day.

00:11:40.633 --> 00:11:43.253
Chunks that break off become icebergs,

00:11:43.303 --> 00:11:45.993
with about 90 percent hidden underwater.

00:11:46.043 --> 00:11:49.033
In 1912, one sank the Titanic,

00:11:49.083 --> 00:11:51.563
and more than 1,500 people died.

00:11:52.313 --> 00:11:54.683
For penguins in the Southern Ocean,

00:11:54.733 --> 00:11:57.193
though, icebergs are handy rafts

00:11:57.243 --> 00:11:58.672
for resting, before they dive back

00:11:58.722 --> 00:11:59.594
in to catch fish.

00:12:00.344 --> 00:12:01.740
In the last ice age,

00:12:01.790 --> 00:12:04.304
sea levels fell about 120 metres,

00:12:04.354 --> 00:12:06.674
and mammoths roamed cold grasslands.

00:12:06.724 --> 00:12:08.030
Today, the ice cap

00:12:08.080 --> 00:12:10.705
on Mount Kilimanjaro is melting fast.

00:12:12.555 --> 00:12:14.890
Beneath our feet lies a hidden world

00:12:14.940 --> 00:12:17.875
of dark caverns and winding tunnels,

00:12:17.925 --> 00:12:19.858
holding some of the most awesome sights

00:12:19.908 --> 00:12:20.785
on the planet.

00:12:21.535 --> 00:12:23.635
Most caves are carved from limestone,

00:12:23.685 --> 00:12:26.495
made from the shells of ancient sea

00:12:26.545 --> 00:12:28.235
creatures, pressed layer by layer

00:12:28.285 --> 00:12:30.356
into rock over millions of years.

00:12:31.106 --> 00:12:33.290
Slightly acidic rainwater dissolves

00:12:33.340 --> 00:12:35.446
the limestone into tunnels.

00:12:35.496 --> 00:12:37.376
Dripping water then grows stalactites

00:12:37.426 --> 00:12:38.646
from the ceiling

00:12:38.696 --> 00:12:40.376
and stalagmites from the floor.

00:12:41.126 --> 00:12:42.781
Sometimes the ground above

00:12:42.831 --> 00:12:43.956
a cave gives way.

00:12:44.006 --> 00:12:45.470
In Guatemala, a sinkhole

00:12:45.520 --> 00:12:48.093
about 30 storeys deep suddenly swallowed

00:12:48.143 --> 00:12:49.667
a three-storey building.

00:12:50.417 --> 00:12:52.727
Cave explorers, called spelunkers,

00:12:52.777 --> 00:12:54.077
love the unknown.

00:12:54.127 --> 00:12:57.007
But they face falling rock, freezing water,

00:12:57.057 --> 00:12:59.947
sudden floods, and getting lost in the dark.

00:13:00.697 --> 00:13:02.906
In Borneo, the Sarawak Chamber

00:13:02.956 --> 00:13:05.207
is the largest cave chamber known.

00:13:05.257 --> 00:13:08.553
And in Vietnam, Hang Sơn Đoòng is so vast

00:13:08.603 --> 00:13:11.038
it has its own jungle and clouds.

00:13:11.788 --> 00:13:13.394
The longest cave system

00:13:13.444 --> 00:13:15.908
on Earth is Mammoth Cave, in Kentucky.

00:13:15.958 --> 00:13:17.860
More than 686 kilometres

00:13:17.910 --> 00:13:19.368
have been mapped,

00:13:19.418 --> 00:13:21.749
and explorers are still finding more.

00:13:23.599 --> 00:13:26.099
Earth's crust is a treasure chest.

00:13:26.149 --> 00:13:27.992
Many things we use every day,

00:13:28.042 --> 00:13:30.959
from talcum powder to the lead in a pencil,

00:13:31.009 --> 00:13:32.929
were formed deep inside it.

00:13:33.679 --> 00:13:36.427
Our buildings are made from it: marble

00:13:36.477 --> 00:13:37.519
for great monuments,

00:13:37.569 --> 00:13:40.329
steel from iron, light aluminium,

00:13:40.379 --> 00:13:42.760
and gypsum for plaster and chalk.

00:13:43.510 --> 00:13:45.130
Our machines run

00:13:45.180 --> 00:13:47.020
on it: copper carries electricity,

00:13:47.070 --> 00:13:48.840
nickel goes into batteries, and liquid

00:13:48.890 --> 00:13:51.680
mercury once filled our thermometers.

00:13:52.430 --> 00:13:53.890
Even your phone depends

00:13:53.940 --> 00:13:55.460
on a mineral called coltan,

00:13:55.510 --> 00:13:57.590
often dug by hand in the Congo,

00:13:57.640 --> 00:14:00.191
in dangerous pits and extreme poverty.

00:14:00.941 --> 00:14:03.311
Some minerals are simply beautiful.

00:14:03.361 --> 00:14:06.421
Fluorite glows under ultraviolet light,

00:14:06.471 --> 00:14:09.721
hematite made the red paint of ancient

00:14:09.771 --> 00:14:12.232
rock art, and one gram of gold can cover

00:14:12.282 --> 00:14:13.351
a square metre.

00:14:14.101 --> 00:14:17.471
Diamond and pencil graphite are both pure

00:14:17.521 --> 00:14:19.237
carbon, just arranged differently.

00:14:19.287 --> 00:14:22.066
The Imperial Crown of India holds

00:14:22.116 --> 00:14:23.922
more than 6,000 diamonds.

00:14:24.672 --> 00:14:27.162
Digging for treasure is dangerous.

00:14:27.212 --> 00:14:29.811
In 2010, the Deepwater Horizon oil rig

00:14:29.861 --> 00:14:32.462
exploded in the Gulf of Mexico, causing

00:14:32.512 --> 00:14:35.793
the largest accidental oil spill in history.

00:14:37.643 --> 00:14:41.133
The deep ocean is the least explored place

00:14:41.183 --> 00:14:43.753
on Earth: dark, freezing and crushing.

00:14:43.803 --> 00:14:46.633
Yet life thrives here, from colossal squid

00:14:46.683 --> 00:14:49.153
to fish that make their own light.

00:14:49.903 --> 00:14:53.623
As we dive, the light fades: the sunlit zone,

00:14:53.673 --> 00:14:57.593
the twilight zone, the dark zone, the abyss,

00:14:57.643 --> 00:15:00.584
and deepest of all, the hadal trenches.

00:15:01.334 --> 00:15:03.604
In the darkness, it snows.

00:15:03.654 --> 00:15:04.917
Tiny bits of dead plants

00:15:04.967 --> 00:15:08.074
and animals drift down as marine snow,

00:15:08.124 --> 00:15:10.804
building thick layers of mud on the seabed.

00:15:11.554 --> 00:15:13.434
Its creatures look alien.

00:15:13.484 --> 00:15:16.514
The Dumbo octopus flaps ear-like fins,

00:15:16.564 --> 00:15:19.207
and giant ostracods glide through the dark

00:15:19.257 --> 00:15:20.895
like floating lanterns.

00:15:21.645 --> 00:15:24.547
On the seafloor, black smokers gush water

00:15:24.597 --> 00:15:27.315
as hot as 400 degrees Celsius,

00:15:27.365 --> 00:15:29.443
and blind shrimp and tube worms gather

00:15:29.493 --> 00:15:31.315
around them like an oasis.

00:15:32.065 --> 00:15:33.214
In 1960, Jacques Piccard

00:15:33.264 --> 00:15:36.156
and Don Walsh rode the submersible

00:15:36.206 --> 00:15:39.075
Trieste nearly 11 kilometres down,

00:15:39.125 --> 00:15:40.174
to the Challenger Deep

00:15:40.224 --> 00:15:41.726
in the Mariana Trench.

00:15:42.476 --> 00:15:45.591
Even by 2022, only 27 people had reached

00:15:45.641 --> 00:15:47.095
that deepest point,

00:15:47.145 --> 00:15:49.469
barely more than the 12 people

00:15:49.519 --> 00:15:51.686
who have walked on the Moon.

00:15:52.436 --> 00:15:54.976
Today, robots do most of the diving,

00:15:55.026 --> 00:15:56.958
mapping underwater mountains

00:15:57.008 --> 00:15:58.216
and valleys with sonar

00:15:58.266 --> 00:16:00.816
that no human eye has ever seen.

00:16:02.666 --> 00:16:05.006
Wind is just moving air,

00:16:05.056 --> 00:16:07.476
invisible and almost weightless.

00:16:07.526 --> 00:16:10.318
Yet it is one of the most destructive forces

00:16:10.368 --> 00:16:11.396
on the planet.

00:16:12.146 --> 00:16:14.946
The Sun heats the Earth unevenly.

00:16:14.996 --> 00:16:17.906
Warm air rises, cool air rushes in, and these

00:16:17.956 --> 00:16:20.896
endless flows create our weather,

00:16:20.946 --> 00:16:23.207
and sometimes, extreme weather.

00:16:23.957 --> 00:16:28.077
When warm, wet air meets cool, dry air,

00:16:28.127 --> 00:16:29.969
a giant spinning storm called

00:16:30.019 --> 00:16:32.247
a supercell can form.

00:16:32.297 --> 00:16:33.717
From its base,

00:16:33.767 --> 00:16:36.387
a tornado reaches down to the ground.

00:16:37.137 --> 00:16:41.239
On 3 April 1974, nearly 150 tornadoes tore

00:16:41.289 --> 00:16:45.807
across the United States in less than a day,

00:16:45.857 --> 00:16:48.158
killing around 300 people.

00:16:48.908 --> 00:16:51.508
The greatest storms have three names:

00:16:51.558 --> 00:16:54.718
hurricanes, typhoons and cyclones.

00:16:54.768 --> 00:16:58.008
Their winds top 250 kilometres per hour,

00:16:58.058 --> 00:17:00.578
and they push deadly storm surges inland.

00:17:01.328 --> 00:17:05.108
In 1900, a hurricane flooded Galveston,

00:17:05.158 --> 00:17:07.508
Texas, killing as many as 10,000 people.

00:17:07.558 --> 00:17:09.983
In 2005, Hurricane Katrina

00:17:10.033 --> 00:17:11.889
devastated New Orleans.

00:17:12.639 --> 00:17:14.781
In Tornado Alley, storm chasers race

00:17:14.831 --> 00:17:16.919
toward storms to collect data.

00:17:16.969 --> 00:17:18.616
Some even drive armoured vehicles

00:17:18.666 --> 00:17:20.320
into the heart of a tornado.

00:17:22.170 --> 00:17:24.928
For centuries, strange weather was blamed

00:17:24.978 --> 00:17:26.440
on angry gods.

00:17:26.490 --> 00:17:28.694
Today, scientists know that most of these

00:17:28.744 --> 00:17:30.680
oddities have natural causes.

00:17:31.430 --> 00:17:32.540
Take clouds.

00:17:32.590 --> 00:17:33.990
They can form mushrooms,

00:17:34.040 --> 00:17:36.280
jellyfish, even donuts.

00:17:36.330 --> 00:17:37.870
And mammatus clouds,

00:17:37.920 --> 00:17:39.130
hanging like pouches,

00:17:39.180 --> 00:17:41.231
warn that a storm is on its way.

00:17:41.981 --> 00:17:44.601
Fish really can fall from the sky.

00:17:44.651 --> 00:17:46.677
Waterspouts suck animals out of rivers

00:17:46.727 --> 00:17:47.841
and lakes, then drop them

00:17:47.891 --> 00:17:50.171
kilometres away in the rain.

00:17:50.921 --> 00:17:53.617
Ice crystals high in thin clouds can wrap

00:17:53.667 --> 00:17:55.358
the Sun in a glowing halo,

00:17:55.408 --> 00:17:57.702
with bright sundogs on either side.

00:17:58.452 --> 00:18:00.482
Hail can be huge.

00:18:00.532 --> 00:18:03.710
In 2010, a hailstone about 20 centimetres

00:18:03.760 --> 00:18:06.822
wide fell in Vivian, South Dakota.

00:18:07.572 --> 00:18:10.427
Lightning strikes about 100 times every

00:18:10.477 --> 00:18:13.407
second, even inside volcanic ash clouds.

00:18:13.457 --> 00:18:16.833
And glowing ball lightning is still a mystery.

00:18:17.583 --> 00:18:19.443
Above thunderstorms,

00:18:19.493 --> 00:18:22.193
red sprites and blue jets flicker.

00:18:22.243 --> 00:18:25.063
And when smoke or ash fills the sky,

00:18:25.113 --> 00:18:27.093
the Moon itself can look blue.

00:18:27.843 --> 00:18:31.033
Flash floods strike with almost no warning.

00:18:31.083 --> 00:18:34.614
In 2010 and 2011, floods in Queensland,

00:18:34.664 --> 00:18:37.359
Australia, covered an area larger

00:18:37.409 --> 00:18:40.104
than France and Germany combined.

00:18:41.954 --> 00:18:43.948
Snow can turn a landscape

00:18:43.998 --> 00:18:46.054
into a white wonderland.

00:18:46.104 --> 00:18:49.182
But in its extreme forms, from blizzards

00:18:49.232 --> 00:18:52.044
to avalanches, it becomes a killer.

00:18:52.794 --> 00:18:53.861
Snowflakes begin

00:18:53.911 --> 00:18:56.754
as tiny six-sided ice crystals,

00:18:56.804 --> 00:18:59.514
then grow branches into complex shapes,

00:18:59.564 --> 00:19:00.882
depending on the temperature

00:19:00.932 --> 00:19:02.375
and moisture in the air.

00:19:03.125 --> 00:19:05.365
Is every snowflake unique?

00:19:05.415 --> 00:19:06.775
Almost certainly.

00:19:06.825 --> 00:19:08.995
With trillions falling every year,

00:19:09.045 --> 00:19:11.975
two identical ones are virtually impossible.

00:19:12.725 --> 00:19:15.935
In the winter of 1998 to 1999, Mount Baker

00:19:15.985 --> 00:19:19.371
in Washington State recorded 29 metres

00:19:19.421 --> 00:19:22.886
of snow, as tall as a ten-storey building.

00:19:23.636 --> 00:19:26.376
When strong winds drive heavy snow,

00:19:26.426 --> 00:19:28.756
a blizzard can become a whiteout, so thick

00:19:28.806 --> 00:19:31.997
that you cannot see your own hand.

00:19:32.747 --> 00:19:35.987
In 1999, near Chamonix in France,

00:19:36.037 --> 00:19:37.202
an avalanche raced down

00:19:37.252 --> 00:19:39.737
at almost 100 kilometres per hour

00:19:39.787 --> 00:19:41.828
and buried a village under snow.

00:19:42.578 --> 00:19:45.438
Rescuers advise: carry a beacon,

00:19:45.488 --> 00:19:48.018
move to the side, grab a tree, swim to stay

00:19:48.068 --> 00:19:51.468
on top, clear a space to breathe,

00:19:51.518 --> 00:19:53.579
and mark where others disappeared.

00:19:55.429 --> 00:19:58.819
In the Arctic, the summer Sun never sets.

00:19:58.869 --> 00:20:00.496
And in winter, there are days

00:20:00.546 --> 00:20:02.470
when it never rises at all.

00:20:03.220 --> 00:20:04.750
In 1958, a submarine sailed

00:20:04.800 --> 00:20:07.380
beneath the North Pole

00:20:07.430 --> 00:20:09.610
and proved there is no land there,

00:20:09.660 --> 00:20:11.341
only floating ice.

00:20:12.091 --> 00:20:14.491
Polar bears hunt seals on the ice,

00:20:14.541 --> 00:20:16.961
Arctic foxes follow them for scraps,

00:20:17.011 --> 00:20:18.171
and beneath the ice,

00:20:18.221 --> 00:20:20.301
divers find surprising life.

00:20:21.051 --> 00:20:22.921
And the cold is brutal.

00:20:22.971 --> 00:20:25.403
In northern Greenland, temperatures can

00:20:25.453 --> 00:20:28.172
drop to about minus 70 degrees Celsius.

00:20:28.922 --> 00:20:30.712
At the other end of the Earth,

00:20:30.762 --> 00:20:34.542
Antarctica lies under the largest ice sheet

00:20:34.592 --> 00:20:35.574
on the planet, holding

00:20:35.624 --> 00:20:37.922
about 70 percent of all its fresh water.

00:20:38.672 --> 00:20:41.128
Its seas teem with penguins,

00:20:41.178 --> 00:20:45.122
seals and whales, all fed by tiny krill.

00:20:45.172 --> 00:20:47.882
In winter, ice shelves spread out,

00:20:47.932 --> 00:20:50.433
and giant icebergs break away.

00:20:51.183 --> 00:20:53.563
At Antarctica's Vostok Station,

00:20:53.613 --> 00:20:55.156
scientists recorded

00:20:55.206 --> 00:20:58.212
the coldest temperature ever measured:

00:20:58.262 --> 00:21:00.384
minus 89.2 degrees Celsius.

00:21:02.234 --> 00:21:05.104
Deserts are the driest places on Earth,

00:21:05.154 --> 00:21:08.004
with scorching days, freezing nights,

00:21:08.054 --> 00:21:10.244
and years without a drop of rain.

00:21:10.994 --> 00:21:13.093
The Sahara is about the size

00:21:13.143 --> 00:21:15.544
of the United States, and still growing.

00:21:15.594 --> 00:21:18.644
Its dunes rise up to 300 metres,

00:21:18.694 --> 00:21:21.455
and dust devils spin across the sand.

00:21:22.205 --> 00:21:25.545
In Sudan, a wall of dust called a haboob

00:21:25.595 --> 00:21:28.225
can swallow a whole market in minutes,

00:21:28.275 --> 00:21:30.675
turning day into orange darkness.

00:21:31.425 --> 00:21:33.555
The official hottest place

00:21:33.605 --> 00:21:35.612
on Earth is Death Valley, in California,

00:21:35.662 --> 00:21:38.720
which reached 56.7 degrees Celsius

00:21:38.770 --> 00:21:40.263
in 1913, after an older record

00:21:40.313 --> 00:21:42.686
in Libya was thrown out.

00:21:43.436 --> 00:21:44.746
And the driest?

00:21:44.796 --> 00:21:46.520
In Chile's Atacama Desert,

00:21:46.570 --> 00:21:48.889
the town of Arica once got less

00:21:48.939 --> 00:21:51.946
than one millimetre of rain in 59 years.

00:21:52.696 --> 00:21:55.577
Yet Las Vegas glitters in the Mojave Desert,

00:21:55.627 --> 00:21:57.506
drinking from the Colorado River,

00:21:57.556 --> 00:21:59.040
and scientists warn

00:21:59.090 --> 00:22:01.437
that its taps could one day run dry.

00:22:02.187 --> 00:22:05.367
In dry lands, fire is never far away.

00:22:05.417 --> 00:22:07.482
Lightning or people spark wildfires

00:22:07.532 --> 00:22:09.413
that race through the bush

00:22:09.463 --> 00:22:10.897
at terrifying speed.

00:22:11.647 --> 00:22:14.463
From fireballs in the sky to the darkness

00:22:14.513 --> 00:22:17.277
of the deepest ocean, our planet is boiling,

00:22:17.327 --> 00:22:20.797
shaking, freezing and changing every day.

00:22:20.847 --> 00:22:22.608
Thank you for watching, and subscribe

00:22:22.658 --> 00:22:25.088
for more wonders of our world.
